%each neural-network model is essential.
Our procedure for estimating $\xi(t) = \E[Y_i(t, \bU_i)]$ at $T_i = t$
is as follows. Denote the observed data by $\cD:=\{\cD_i\}_{i=1}^N$
where $\cD_i := \{Y_i, T_i, \bR_i, \bm{Z}_i\}$.  We use the following
$K$-fold cross-fitting procedure, assuming that $N$ is divisible by
$K$.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Randomly partition the data into $K$ folds of equal size where the size of each fold is $n = N/K$.  The observation index is denoted by $I(i)\in \{1,\dots,K\}$ where $I(i)=k$ implies that the $i$th observation belongs to the $k$th fold.
    \item For each fold $k \in \{1, \cdots, K\}$, use observations with $I(i)\ne k$ as training data:
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item split the training data into two folds, $I_1^{(-k)}$ and $I_2^{(-k)}$
    \item estimate both the deconfounder and the conditional outcome
      function by solving the optimization problem given in
      Equation~\eqref{loss_text_original} using the first fold.  They
      are denoted by
      $\hat\boldf^{(-k)}(\{\bR_i, \bm{Z}_i\}_{i \in
        I_1^{(-k)}}):=\boldf(\{\bR_i, \bm{Z}_i\}_{i \in I_1^{(-k)}};
      \hat\blambda^{(-k)})$ and
      $\hat{\mu}_t^{(-k)}(\{\bR_i, \bm{Z}_i\}_{i \in
        I_1^{(-k)}}):=\\\ \mu_t(\boldf(\{\bR_i\}_{i \in I_1^{(-k)}},
      \bm{Z}_i; \hat\blambda^{(-k)}); \hat\btheta^{(-k)})$,
      respectively, and
    \item obtain an estimated propensity score given the estimated deconfounder on the second fold, which is denoted by $\hat{\pi}^{(-k)}(\hat\boldf^{(-k)}(\{\bR_i\}_{i \in I_2^{(-k)}}), \bm{Z}_i):=\hat{\pi}^{(-k)} (\boldf(\{\bR_i\}_{i \in I_2^{(-k)}}; \hat\blambda^{(-k)}), \bm{Z}_i)$.
    \end{enumerate}
  \item For each treatment value $t \in \cT$, construct the double debiased ML estimator
  $$
  \hat{\xi}(t) \ = \ \frac{1}{nK} \sum_{k = 1}^K \sum_{I(i) = k} \biggl\{ \hat{\mu}_t^{(-k)}(\boldf(\bR_i), \bm{Z}_i) + \frac{ \mathbbm{1}\{T_i = t\}}{\hat{\pi}_t^{(-k)}(\boldf(\bR_i), \bm{Z}_i)} \biggl( Y_i - \hat{\mu}_t^{(-k)}(\boldf(\bR_i), \bm{Z}_i) \biggr) \biggr\}
  $$
  %where $K_h(x) = \frac{3}{4h} \{1-(\frac{x}{h})^2\}\mathbbm{1}\{|\frac{x}{h}| \leq 1\}$ is a Epanechnikov kernel function
  %with some bandwidth parameter $h$.
\end{enumerate}
Under the standard regularity conditions and the Lipschitz continuity
of the propensity score function, the procedure above yields the
asymptotically normal estimator with the double robustness property. %\citep{colangelo2025double}.

\subsection{Implementation Details for the Empirical Application}

We reanalyze the dataset originally compiled by
\citet{lindholm2024facial}. In that study, the authors collected
facial photographs of Danish political candidates, along with their
vote totals and background characteristics --- including date of
birth, gender, and education --- for the 2021 local and 2022 general
elections. The initial dataset contained 9,020 candidates. However,
the authors excluded individuals whose photos were missing, of low
quality, or overlaid with text, as well as those who ran
uncontested. This resulted in a final analytic sample of 7,055
candidates. To quantify facial traits, the authors fine-tuned a
pre-trained convolutional neural network using the ``One Million
Impressions'' dataset \citep{peterson2022deep}, generating estimates
for three dimensions of perceived facial characteristics:
attractiveness, dominance, and trustworthiness.


% They divided the dataset into training (60\%), validation (20\%),
% and test (20\%) sets. The CNN was trained on the training set, with
% the validation set used for early stopping, and its performance was
% evaluated on the test set using mean absolute error (MAE) and
% Pearson’s correlation coefficient. The final model achieved MAE
% values of 0.732, 0.602, and 0.576, and correlation coefficients of
% 0.787, 0.756, and 0.754 for attractiveness, dominance, and
% trustworthiness, respectively. The fine-tuned CNN then predicted
% these facial traits for all candidates, which served as the
% independent variables of interest.

We implemented our proposed methodology as follows. First, we padded
each image to a square shape with dimensions of $304 \times 304$
pixels to ensure uniform input size. We then regenerated the candidate
images using Stable Diffusion version~1.5
\citep{rombach_high-resolution_2022}. We extracted the internal
representation from the final layer of the diffusion model, prior to
the decoder of the variational autoencoder. Consistent with
Assumption~\ref{det_dec}, we made sure that the decoder is a
deterministic function of this internal representation.

Unlike in the text-based application, we did not apply a pooling
operation to reduce dimensionality. Instead, we flattened the full
internal representation (a tensor of shape $16 \times 16 \times 64$)
into a single vector of dimension 16,384, which is substantially
smaller than the original image size of
$304 \times 304 \times 3 = 277,248$.

We estimated both the deconfounder and the outcome model, using this
