We need also continuity in the first argument, corresponding to (\ref{A8}). We use that, for $x\in\R^n\setminus K$, the bodies $K$ and $x+ d^E(K,x)(-E)$ touch, that is, they have a nonempty intersection but can be separated by a hyperplane. This implies, for $x\in\R^n\setminus K_0$,
\begin{equation}\label{A9}
K_j\to K_0 \Rightarrow d^E(K_j,x)\to d^E(K_0,x).
\end{equation}
In fact, the sequence $(d^E(K_j,x))_{j\in \N}$ has a convergent subsequence. If some convergent subsequence had a limit different from $d^E(K_0,x)$, then there would be numbers $j$ for which $x+ d^E(K_j,x)(-E)$ does not touch $K_j$, a contradiction. Assertion (\ref{A9}) follows.

Next we show that
\begin{equation}\label{A10}
K_j\to K_0 \Rightarrow p^E(K_j,x)\to p^E(K_0,x).
\end{equation}
For the proof, denote by $\delta_j$ the Hausdorff distance between $K_j$ and $K_0$. Then the ball with center $p^E(K_0,x)$ and radius $\delta_j$ meets $K_j$. It follows from this fact and from (\ref{A5}) that any convergent subsequence of the sequence $(p^E(K_j,x))_{j\in\N}$ converges to a point in the hyperplane that separates $x+d^E(K_0,x)(-E)$ and $K_0$. Since $E$ is strictly convex, this point is equal to $p^E(K_0,x)$. Therefore, (\ref{A10}) follows.

If $K_j\to K_0$, $x_j\in {\rm bd}\,K_j$ for $j\in\N_0$, $x_j\to x_0$ and $u_j$ is an outer unit normal vector of $K_j$ at $x_j$ for $j\in\N$, then $u_0$ is a normal vector of $K_0$ at $x_0$. Since $E$ is regular, it follows that
\begin{equation}\label{A11}
K_j\to K_0 \Rightarrow u^E(K_j,x)\to u^E(K_0,x).
\end{equation}

