To address these issues, we propose \toolname{}, which helps improve LLM-based APR by utilizing a multi-round iterative tree search method combined with CoT and self-evaluation to generate patches. 
Unlike the trial-and-error repair paradigm, we adopt an \textbf{evaluate-and-improve} approach to guide the model toward the correct repair path. Through effective global patch evaluation, \toolname{} can quickly discover mistakes and backtrack from incorrect repair paths, step by step approaching the correct patch by restarting from the most promising candidate.
For \toolname{}, each iteration of patch search can be divided into four stages: \textbf{Patch Selection}, \textbf{Patch Generation}, \textbf{Patch Evaluation}, \textbf{Patch Tree Updating}. In the patch selection stage, \toolname{} first selects an explored patch from the patch tree according to the UCT (Upper Confidence Bounds Applied to Trees) value. Then in the patch generation stage, \toolname{} inspires LLMs to perform repairs on selected patch through CoT, and further conducts self-reflection on the generated patches. 
In the patch evaluation stage, the generated patches are validated for correctness on test cases. For those patches that fail the tests, \toolname{} assesses their quality and then add them to the patch tree. Specifically, we adopt \textbf{LLM-as-Judge} and \textbf{Test-as-Judge} strategies adaptively for evaluation based on whether the test cases are sufficient.
In the patch tree updating stage, back propagation is performed from the selected patch upwards to the root node of the patch tree. 
After a certain number of iterations (16 and 32 in our work), \toolname{} outputs all the plausible patches found for patch validation. 
% \toolname{} consists of two key modules: \textbf{Patch Generator} for generating patches and \textbf{Patch Evaluator} for evaluating the generated patches. 
% Notably, any LLM can be substituted for the two modules which enhances the framework's flexibility.

% To optimize computational resources, we implement a self-evaluation strategy where the same LLM serves as both the patch generator and the patch evaluator.



Compared with prior LLM-based APR techniques, \toolname{} has the following advantages. 
\textbf{(1) Global optimum.} 
During each iteration of repair, \toolname{} selects the highest-scoring patch from the patch search space as the starting point for the next search. 
Compared with approaches that focus solely on a single patch, \toolname{} ensures the global optimum of the search algorithm. 
In terms of results, \toolname{} can fix 201 out of 835 bugs on Defects4J, surpassing all 9 state-of-the-art baselines.
\textbf{(2) Flexibility and generality.} \toolname{} is flexible as it works seamlessly with any LLMs. 
\toolname{} is also generalizable to different search algorithms.
Although we adopt the representative Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) to demonstrate the effectiveness of \toolname{}, it can be replaced by any other search algorithm (e.g., genetic algorithms).
\textbf{(3) High search efficiency, larger search space with fewer search iterations.} 
Compared with serial single-path methods, \toolname{} employs a multi-path search strategy to generate more diverse patches. By pruning low-quality search paths, \toolname{} avoids straying too far down incorrect trajectories, thereby reducing the number of search iterations.
For example, \toolname{} adopts a smaller patch size (16 and 32) than that used in previous studies (10000~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/JiangL021}, 500~\cite{DBLP:conf/issta/Xia024}).

This paper makes the following contributions:

\begin{itemize}
    \item We propose \toolname{}, which utilizes tree search to optimize the LLM-based APR process, representing a new technological endeavor in the field of APR.
    \toolname{} offers multiple advantages, such as flexible architecture, preferable effectiveness, and efficiency.

    \item We evaluate \toolname{} against 9 state-of-the-art baselines (including learning-based, template-based and LLM-based APR techniques) and 13 representative LLMs. Experimental results show that \toolname{} outperforms existing baselines, fixing 108 and 93 bugs on Defects4J-v1.2 and Defects4J-v2, respectively. 

     \item We implement \toolname{} with seven best-performing LLMs.
     The results show that \toolname{} can fix 20\% more bugs compared to vanilla LLMs on average, demonstrating its model-agnostic nature in enhancing the APR capabilities of diverse LLMs.

    \item We validate the multi-language (Python/Java) and multi-type (Repository/Competition) bug repair capability of \toolname{} on ConDefects. We find that \toolname{} takes less than 20\% of the time cost and less than 50\% of the monetary cost compared to ChatRepair~\cite{DBLP:conf/issta/Xia024}.

\end{itemize}

\section{Background and Related Work}
\textbf{Automated Program Repair (APR)} aims to assist developers in localizing and fixing program bugs automatically. 
Traditional APR techniques can be classified as heuristic-based~\cite{Simfix:2018,10.1145/3468264.3468600}, constraint-based~\cite{196,106} and template-based~\cite{44,45}. 
% Template-based APR is recognized as the most effective one. However, it cannot fix bugs that go beyond the rules defined by templates. 
Modern APR methods, primarily based on deep learning, have improved upon the shortcomings of previous APR methods. 
Learning-based methods~\cite{VulRepair,49,TransR} strike a balance between performance and effectiveness while offering stronger generalization capabilities.
As part of learning-based methods, Neural Machine Translation (NMT) techniques have
been extensively studied in recent years, e.g., TENURE~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/MengWZSLH23}, Tare~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/ZhuSZXZ23},
SelfAPR~\cite{DBLP:conf/kbse/YeML0M22}, RewardRepair~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/YeMM22}, Recoder~\cite{DBLP:conf/sigsoft/ZhuSXZY0Z21} and CURE~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/JiangL021} . They share the same insight that APR can be viewed as an NMT problem that aims to translate buggy code into correct code.
One downside of NMT-based methods is that they relatively sensitive to noise in the training dataset. To improve this, LLM-based methods~\cite{DBLP:conf/sigsoft/XiaZ22,DBLP:conf/iclr/FriedAL0WSZYZL23,DBLP:conf/kbse/XiaDZ23} leverages the general capabilities of LLMs in code-related tasks, enabling them to fix bugs through zero-shot or few-shot methods, thereby reducing the dependence on high-quality training datasets. Xia et al.~\cite{DBLP:conf/icse/XiaWZ23} conducted an extensive study of LLM-based APR
techniques based on various LLMs (e.g., Codex~\cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2107-03374}, GPT-NeoX~\cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2204-06745}, CodeT5~\cite{DBLP:conf/emnlp/0034WJH21}, InCoder~\cite{DBLP:conf/iclr/FriedAL0WSZYZL23}),  demonstrating the superiority of LLM-based APR. More recently, ChatRepair~\cite{DBLP:conf/issta/Xia024} utilizes GPT-3.5 to fix bugs and obtains state-of-the-art results. Our work thoroughly investigates various types of modern LLMs and comprehensively evaluates their capacities of fixing bugs. Building upon this foundation, we draw inspiration from previous works~\cite{DBLP:journals/tse/GouesNFW12,DBLP:conf/icse/YeM24} and adopt an iterative algorithm to optimize the performance of LLMs on APR. We employ a search-based approach, integrating LLMs with the Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) algorithm. 
The method we propose, \toolname{}, can serve as a LLM-based APR framework that suits variable LLMs.

\textbf{Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS)} is used to enhance decision-making capabilities in complex scenarios and shows significant results in strategy games such as Go. MCTS is a multi-round iterative algorithm, each round generally involves four key phases~\cite{DBLP:journals/tciaig/BrownePWLCRTPSC12}: Selection, based on UCT strategy to identify a potential starting point for exploration; expansion, where new nodes are added; evaluation, to evaluate the newly expanded nodes; and back propagation, updating the node values based on evaluation results.
Compared to other search methods, such as Depth-First Search (DFS) and Breadth-First Search (BFS), which tend to suffer from disadvantages like getting trapped in local errors and having a massive search space, MCTS can strike a balance between efficiency and effectiveness. 
Previous work (MathBlackBox~\cite{zhang2024accessing}) uses MCTS to guide GPT-4 in solving Olympic-level Math problems. 
Recently, researchers~\cite{dainese2024generatingcodeworldmodels,li2024rethinkmctsrefiningerroneousthoughts,delorenzo2024makecountllmbasedhighquality} have found that MCTS helps improve the efficiency of code-related tasks such as code generation, test generation, and program debugging. 
% MCTS can efficiently plan path searches in code-related tasks, while also autonomously refining its strategies by leveraging appropriately designed and effective reward algorithms.
% Our work draws from MCTS, integrating tree search methodologies and APR in the era of LLMs.
Although our work is general to different search algorithms, we implement it with MCTS by interactive tree search.
This approach enables LLMs to iteratively select, search, and evaluate patches, resulting in the generation of a higher number of correct patches at a lower cost.

