To evaluate the impact of the search and evaluation components on the overall effectiveness of \toolname{}, we compare its performance against CoT-enhanced and vanilla LLM baselines.
As shown in Table~\ref{detailed_results}, it can be observed that, all seven LLMs demonstrate improved effectiveness with \toolname{} compared to using only CoT and Vanilla LLMs.
In particular, GPT-3.5 (\toolname{}) fixes 20 more bugs than GPT-3.5 (CoT), GPT-4o-mini (\toolname{}) fixes 27 more bugs than GPT-4o-mini (CoT), Yi-Coder-9B (\toolname{}) fixes six more bugs than Yi-Coder-9B (CoT). Furthermore, with the patch size increasing to 32, GPT-3.5 with \toolname{} can fix 42 additional bugs. 

When comparing the performance of LLMs of different sizes, we find that large-scale models like GPT-4o-mini, GPT-3.5, Yi-Coder-9B and Qwen2.5-Coder-7B show more significant improvement, compared to smaller models such as Qwen2.5-Coder-3B and Stable-Code-3B. For GPT-4o-mini and GPT-3.5, 90\% (27/30) and 74\% (20/27) of the overall improvement in bug-fix is attributed to search and evaluation when comparing \toolname{} to Vanilla LLMs, respectively. For Yi-Coder-9B, Qwen2.5-Coder-7B, Qwen2.5-Coder-3B, and Stable-Code-3B, this proportion is 16\% (6/37), 28.5\% (8/28), 36\% (4/11), and 50\% (2/4), respectively.
It indicates that large-scale models benefit more from searching compared to small-scale models. This is because large-scale models are more accurate in patch evaluation, and accurate evaluation helps guide the search in the right direction. 

We also observe that as patch size increases, search and evaluation start playing a more significant role. For Llama-3.1-8B , when patch size is between 8 and 12, the number of bug-fixes by \toolname{} is slightly lower than that of CoT. However, as patch size increases, the performance of \toolname{} gradually ties that of CoT (when patch size = 14) and then surpasses it (when patch size $>$ 14). Qwen2.5-Coder-3B exhibits the same trend, with \toolname{} outperforming CoT when patch size exceeds 14. It indicates that as patch size increases, \toolname{} is able to resolve more complex bugs that other methods cannot solve. 

\finding{3}{
All components, including test information, CoT, search, and evaluation, have a positive effect on \toolname{}. 
Among them, test information is effective for all LLMs (e.g., helping GPT-4o-mini fix 21 more bugs). CoT is effective for 6/7 LLMs (e.g., helping Yi-Coder-9B fix 31 more bugs). Search and evaluation are effective for all LLMs (e.g., helping GPT-4o-mini repair 30 more bugs). 
Moreover, large-scale models provide more accurate evaluations of patch quality, leading to better search results (e.g., GPT-3.5 fixes 27 more bugs, while Qwen2.5-Coder-3B only fixes 8 more bugs).
