\subsection{RQ5: Effectiveness of Large Patch Size}

\textbf{Experimental Design.} 
RQ 1-4 have demonstrated \toolname{}'s effectiveness with small patch size (e.g., 16, 32). To further investigate the impact of large patch size, we select GPT-3.5 for extreme testing. We increase the patch size from 32 to 500 (50 iterations, 10 patches per iteration) to align with ChatRepair's configuration.

\input{tab/patchsize_500_extra_fix}

\textbf{Results and Analysis.}
We list the newly fixed bugs in Table~\ref{extra_fix_patch_500}, where $\checkmark$ represents a correct fix, and \texttimes \ represents a plausible but not correct fix.
It can be observed that a larger patch size (500) leads to more plausible fixes (16) and correct fixes (11). However, as the patch size increases, the number of newly fixed bugs significantly decreases. This indicates that \toolname{} has already approaches its upper limit.

\finding{5}{
A larger patch size (32 $\rightarrow$ 500) helps \toolname{} fix 11 more bugs, suggesting that increased search budget further enhances its repair effectiveness.
