\section{Threats to Validity}
\label{sec:threats}

\textbf{Internal Threat.}
The main internal threat involves the potential of data leakage in \toolname{}.
To address this concern, we employ several strategies.
First, we implement \toolname{} with a diverse set of LLMs, including two black-box models (GPT-3.5 and GPT-4o-mini) and five open-source models (e.g., Qwen and LLaMA). 
For the open-source models, we carefully examine their pre-training datasets (e.g., CodeSearchNet) and confirm that there is no overlap with benchmarks. 
Second, some of the models are also adopted by baselines (e.g., ChatRepair). 
The consistent performance improvements achieved by \toolname{} in these cases, despite relying on the same underlying models, indicate that the gains are attributable to the design of our framework rather than memorization by LLMs. 
Third, as shown in RQ2, \toolname{} consistently enhances performance across different backbone models, further supporting its model-agnostic effectiveness. 
Lastly, following prior work~\cite{DBLP:conf/issta/Xia024}, we include the ConDefects dataset in our evaluation to further mitigate the risk of data leakage. 
Thus, we are confident that data leakage does not pose a significant threat to the validity of our findings.


\textbf{External Threat.}
The main external threat to validity lies in the adoption of benchmarks. 
The performance of \toolname{} may not generalize to other datasets.
To mitigate this, we evaluate \toolname{} on both repository-level bugs (e.g., Defects4J) and competition-level bugs (e.g., ConDefects).
Moreover, \toolname{} is agnostic to bug types and programming languages, enabling its direct integration into a wide range of datasets.
Therefore, we believe this threat has a minimal impact on our conclusions, and \toolname{} has the potential to handle more complex and diverse bugs. 
% In the future, we will explore the performance of \toolname{} on higher-difficulty defect datasets by combining repository-level context extraction techniques (e.g., knowledge graphs) and agent technologies (e.g., AutoCodeRover~\cite{zhang2024autocoderover}).




\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we introduce \toolname{} that employs iterative tree search to improve LLM-based APR.
\toolname{} employs the following strategies: 
(1) incorporate MCTS into the patch search process to enhance efficiency and effectiveness.
(2) Perform global evaluation on explored patches to avoid falling into local optima.
Our experiments on 835 bugs from Defects4J demonstrate that \toolname{} can fix a total of 201 bugs, which outperforms the other nine state-of-the-art baselines. We further demonstrate \toolname{}'s multi-lingual and multi-type bug fixing ability on ConDefects-Python. Compared to existing LLM-based APR tools, the time and monetary costs of \toolname{} are less than 20\% and 50\%, 

