State-of-the-art compression packages often combine different compression algorithms and run them efficiently on parallel CPU cores and vector units. As shown in Table~\ref{table:compression_libraries}, we study zstd~\cite{FacebookZstd}, snappy~\cite{GoogleSnappy}, and lz4~\cite{YannColletLZ4}, zLib~\cite{gailly2004zlib}, and  bzip~\cite{seward1996bzip2} working on multicore CPUs and nvCOMP-LZ4~\cite{nvcomp} for GPUs as representatives of efficient combinations of dictionary and entropy coders. The selected compression tools are a combination of run-length encoding (RLE) and a dictionary-based coding and/or an entropy coder such as Huffman coding~\cite{huffman1952method} and benefit from common data transformation. 

