\subsection{Ablations}
We assess the contribution of key components through an ablation study, using recorded camera paths across all scenes (see supplemental video). Table~\ref{tab:Ablations} reports average frame times over these paths.
Caching significantly improves performance, roughly doubling the framerate across scenes. Frustum culling is particularly beneficial for large-scale models but may incur overhead in smaller scenes. To evaluate cut efficiency, we compare the time required to compute the visible set using either full hierarchy BFS or our HSPT-based approach. HSPT consistently yields faster cut times.
For training, we measure average iteration durations over 1\,000 steps. Here, frustum culling again proves essential, substantially reducing the number of Gaussians loaded and processed per view.

\paragraph{View Selection}
On the MatrixCity-Scale dataset (250K iterations, up to 60M Gaussians), enabling view selection reduces the number of Gaussians loaded from RAM per frame from 355K to 219K---a $35\%$ reduction in memory load. Reconstruction quality is unaffected, with PSNR and SSIM improving slightly by 0.04 and 0.05, respectively.


%We conducted an extensive ablation study to quantify the contribution of key components in our system. For each scene, we recorded a camera path spanning representative viewpoints; these trajectories are available in the supplemental video. Table~\ref{tab:Ablations} reports average frame times over the full path.

%The caching mechanism substantially improves performance, yielding a $2\times$ increase in framerate across all test cases. Frustum culling further reduces rendering times for large scenes by eliminating off-screen geometry early in the hierarchy traversal. However, for smaller scenes, the associated overhead can outweigh its benefits. To evaluate cut computation efficiency, we compare the runtime of a full hierarchy breadth-first search (BFS) against our proposed HSPT-based approach. Across all settings, HSPT consistently achieves faster cut computation.
%For training performance, we measure average iteration durations over 1\,000 steps. We find that frustum culling is particularly critical during training, where it significantly reduces the number of Gaussians transferred and processed per frame.

%\paragraph{View Selection}
%On the MS-Scale dataset, we train for 250{\,}000 iterations with a densified model containing up to 60 million Gaussians. Enabling view selection reduces the average number of Gaussians streamed from RAM per frame from 355{\,}770 to 219{\,}043, corresponding to a $35\%$ reduction in memory load time. Importantly, this optimization does not degrade reconstruction quality; PSNR and SSIM metrics increase marginally by 0.04 and 0.05, respectively.









\section{Discussion and Outlook}
\emph{A LoD of Gaussians} enables seamless training and rendering of ultra-large 3DGS models on consumer hardware. By storing Gaussian data in external memory and streaming it on demand, our method avoids the pitfalls of chunk-based pipelines. The hierarchical SPT structure accelerates LoD selection and remains robust to ongoing training changes. Combined with caching and view selection, our system significantly reduces out-of-core overhead. Together, these components enable efficient reconstruction and rendering at scale, demonstrated on the MatrixCity-Scale dataset.

\paragraph{Limitations and Future Work}
A primary limitation of our method lies in initialization. Accurate estimation of camera poses and sparse point clouds remains challenging at large scale, particularly for real-world datasets with sparse or inconsistent coverage. Future work could explore improved initialization strategies or joint optimization of poses and Gaussians during early training.

Our system also requires roughly 1~GB of RAM per million Gaussians, which---while more efficient than prior methods---still constrains scalability. Loading from disk is feasible in our experiments, though at the cost of a $10\times$ slowdown. %Online compression of Gaussian parameters may further reduce memory usage.
Preliminary exploration has also shown that given access to sufficient RAM and training time, our method could scale far beyond what is presented here. A 150 million Gaussian model has been trained successfully for MatrixCity-Scale on 24\,GB of VRAM, though the improvement to image quality was minor. We suspect that even more training views are required to properly leverage such a vast number of Gaussians. 


Rendering could be further optimized by avoiding per-frame hierarchy cut recomputation and enabling asynchronous streaming. While frustum culling reduces memory load in most views, it is ineffective when the entire scene falls inside the frustum. Occlusion culling could address this by skipping entire SPTs before loading.

Overall, we believe that out-of-core 3D Gaussian Splatting is a promising direction for scaling radiance field methods to city-scale and beyond, without the need for specialized hardware.




%\emph{A LoD of Gaussians} enables seamless training and rendering of ultra-large 3DGS models on consumer-grade hardware. It bypasses problems with the conventional divide-and-conquer approach by storing the Gaussian properties in external memory and loading them on demand. The novel hierarchical SPT datastructure not only accelerates LoD rendering, but is also robust against changes during training. A specialised caching and view selection system further reduce the overhead of using out-of-core memory. This overhauled pipeline simplifies, accelerates and improves large scale reconstruction and rendering. We demonstrated our technique on the expanded MatrixCity-Scale dataset.

%\paragraph{Limitations and Future Work}

%The main limitation of our method currently lies in the initialization, as both camera poses and a sparse point cloud are difficult to accurately compute for datasets of this scale, limiting its full power to synthetic datasets for the time being. More work is required to properly initialize 3D Gaussian Splatting from large sets of sparse-view real-world photographs. 

%Another obstacle in the use of this method is the amount of RAM required to run it, with about 1 GB being required per 1 million Gaussians trained. While we feel that this is still a significant improvement over the industrial-scale GPUs that were required for previous methods, this does limit the possible training size on consumer hardware. 
%If increasing the training times by a factor of roughly 10 is not an issue, Gaussians can be loaded from disk instead of RAM, allowing almost arbitrary model size. Compressing the Gaussians during training may also help.

%We also see great potential in further optimizing the renderer. For example, the hierarchy cut is currently being recomputed every time, even though it barely changes from frame to frame. Streaming the Gaussians asynchronously from RAM would also lead to a smoother experience. While our frustum culling reduces the amount of Gaussians considerably for most views, in the worst case the entire scene may be contained in the frustum. For such cases, a form of occlusion culling could be beneficial to cull entire SPTs before they have to be loaded.

%Overall, we believe that using out-of-core memory for Gaussian splatting is an exciting research direction with great potential and is essential to break the reliance of large-scale 3DGS on industrial-scale GPUs.



