\section{Introduction}\label{sec:Intro}

Gardner and Jackson \cite{2008_gardner_jackson} showed that the ordered monoid generated under composition
by closure $c$ and interior $i$ on a given nonempty topological space $X$ is always one of the six in Figure~\ref{fig:gj}
where $o_1\leq o_2$ if and only if $o_1A\su o_2A$ for all $A\in\mathcal{P}(X)$ and $\ident$ is the identity
operator.
They named the extension that includes complementary duals after Kuratowski, who discovered it over a century ago \cite{1922_kuratowski}.

Ciraulo \cite{2025_ciraulo} recently studied the analogue of Figure~\ref{fig:gj} for
two idempotent, non-decreasing operators $c,i$ on an arbitrary poset subject to only
the usual closure and interior relations $i\leq\ident\leq c$
without any duality assumptions involving complementation.
Using results from \cite{2025_ciraulo}, in Section~\ref{sec:monoids} we will prove:

\begin{thm}\label{thm:monoids}
If \mth{$c,i$} are general closure and interior operators on a given poset \mth{$(L,\leq)$}
as described above\nit, then they generate one of \mth{$18$} different operator monoids under composition
\nit(all of which occur\nit{).}
\end{thm}

Since complement is not available, it is natural to call these monoids the \textit{Kuratowski monoids\/} of $(L,\leq)$
and their elements \textit{Kuratowski operators\/}.
In our proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:monoids}, functions on small finite posets will be represented by diagrams such as the
following one, which represents the closure function $f$ on $(\{1,2,3\},\leq)$ defined by $f(1)=1$ and $f(2)=f(3)=3$:	

