\section{The Kuratowski Monoid of a Poset}\label{sec:monoids}

The following lemma is clear.

\begin{lem}\label{lem:duality}
Since every closure \nit(interior\nit) operator on \mth{$(L,\leq)$} is an interior \nit(closure\nit) operator on its dual
\mth{$(L,\geq)$,}
any equivalence between two Boolean combinations of equations among Kuratowski operators
is true for all posets if and only if its dual\nit, obtained by interchanging closure and interior\nit, is also true for all
posets.
Thus every poset has both a Kuratowski monoid and a dual Kuratowski monoid \nit(possibly the same\nit{).}
\end{lem}

The next lemma follows immediately from Propositions~$2.1$ and~$2.3$ in \cite{2025_ciraulo}.

\begin{lem}\label{lem:imps}
The Kuratowski monoid of \mth{$(L,\leq)$} satisfies the Hasse diagram on the left in Figure~\nit{\ref{fig:gj}}
and the edges hold in the Hasse diagram at the bottom of Figure~\nit{\ref{fig:monoids}.}
\end{lem}

We are now ready to prove Theorem~\ref{thm:monoids}.
The following equivalences will be used:
$cic=\ident\ \Longleftrightarrow\ c=i$ and
$ic=ci\ \Longleftrightarrow\ cic=ici$ (see \cite{2025_ciraulo}).

\bigskip\noindent
\textit{Proof\/}.
It is well known \cite{1922_kuratowski}
that the Kuratowski monoid of $(\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}),\su)$ under the usual topology
satisfies no equation in Figure~\ref{fig:monoids}.
Setting $c(1)=i(1)=1$ on $(\{1\},\es)$ satisfies $c=i$.
Setting $c(1)=i(1)=i(2)=1$ and $c(2)=2$ on $(\{1,2\},\leq)$ satisfies $c=\ident$ and $c\neq i$.
By the lemmas above and Figure~\ref{fig:monoids}, at least $15$ more Kuratowski monoids exist, giving us a total of at least $18$.

By Lemma~$2.2$ in \cite{2025_ciraulo}, to complete the proof it suffices to show that when $(L,\leq)$\
satisfies any two unrelated equations in Figure~\ref{fig:monoids}, its Kuratowski monoid is represented by one of the
$17$ labeled boxes.

Since the equation $c=i$ is related to every equation in Figure~\ref{fig:monoids}, it can be ruled out.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Case 1.} $c=\ident$. Since
$(c=\ident\mbox{ and }c=cic)\ \Longleftrightarrow\  cic=\ident\ \Longleftrightarrow\ c=i$, no new monoids occur.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Case 2.} $cic=i$. Obviously,
$(cic=i\mbox{ and }c=cic)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ c=i$.

\medskip\noindent
By Lemma~\ref{lem:duality} we can rule out the duals of Cases~$1$ and~$2$, namely, $\ident=i$ and $c=ici$.
The equations $ic=i$ and $ci=i$ get ruled out since

\bigskip
{
\centering
\begin{tabular}{rcl}
$(ic=i\mbox{ and }c=cic)$&$\Longleftrightarrow$&$(ic=i\mbox{ and }c=c(ic)=c(i))$,\\
$(ci=i\mbox{ and }c=cic)$&$\Longleftrightarrow$&$(ci=i\mbox{ and }c=(ci)c=(i)c)$,\\
$(ic=i\mbox{ and }ci=ici)$&$\Longleftrightarrow$&$((ic=i\mbox{ and }ici=i)\mbox{ and }ci=ici)$\\
&$\Longleftrightarrow$&$(ci=i\mbox{ and }ic=ici)$\\
&$\Longleftrightarrow$&$(ci=i\mbox{ and }(ci)c=(i)c=ici)$\\
&$\Longleftrightarrow$&$cic=i$.\\
\end{tabular}

}

