\noindent
By Lemma~\ref{lem:duality}, the duals of the equivalences above rule out $c=ci$ and $c=ic$.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Case 3.} $cic=ici$. The equivalence
$(cic=ici\mbox{ and }ici=i)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ cic=i$ and its dual rule out this case.

\medskip\noindent
\textbf{Case 4.} $ici=i$. Obviously, $(ici=i\mbox{ and }ic=ici)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ ic=i$, and
$(ici=i\mbox{ and }ci=ici)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ ci=i$.

\medskip\noindent
By Lemma~\ref{lem:duality} we can rule out the dual of Case~$4$, namely, $c=cic$.

\medskip\noindent
The only equations left, $ic=ici$ and $ci=ici$, get ruled out simultaneously by the equivalences
\[
(ic=ici\mbox{ and }ci=ici)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ ci=ic\ \Longleftrightarrow\ cic=ici.\eqno{\qed}
\]

