\section{The Interior-Pseudocomplement Problem}\label{sec:hasse}

After showing that for an arbitrary poset $(L,\leq)$
interior and \textit{pseudocomplement\/}---defined to be any function ${-}:L\to L$ such that
$x\leq{-}y\ \Longleftrightarrow\ y\leq{-}x$ for all $x,y\in L$---constructively generate at most $31$ distinct
operators under composition,
Ciraulo \cite{2025_ciraulo} proposed without proof that Figure~\ref{fig:ip-monoid} is the Hasse diagram
of all inequalities these $31$ operators satisfy in general.
We prove this now.

\begin{prop}
Figure~\nit{\ref{fig:ip-monoid}} gives the Hasse diagram of the interior-pseudocomplement monoid for an
arbitrary poset \mth{$(L,\leq)$} where \mth{$b:={-}i{-}$.}
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
First we verify that all $46$ edges hold.
Edge $6$, $\ident\leq{-}{-}$, holds by reflexivity of $\leq$ and the definition of pseudocomplement:
\[
x\leq{-}({-}x)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ ({-}x)\leq{-}x.
\]
Thus ${-}$ is antitone, for we have
\[
x\leq y\ \Longrightarrow\ x\leq y\leq{-}({-}y)\ \Longleftrightarrow\ ({-}y)\leq {-}x.
\]
Applying this to $\ident\leq{-}{-}$ yields ${-}{-}{-}\leq{-}$; right-multiplying $\ident\leq{-}{-}$ by
${-}$ yields ${-}\leq{-}{-}{-}$. Hence ${-}{-}{-}={-}$.
Right-multiplying this equation by ${-}$, we conclude that ${-}{-}$ is a closure operator on $L$.

By definition $b={-}i{-}$.
Left-multiplying the two inequalities ${-}{-}i{-}\leq{-}$ (which holds since
$i\leq\ident\ \Longrightarrow\ i{-}\leq{-}\ \Longrightarrow\ {-}{-}\leq{-}i{-}$)
and ${-}{-}(i{-})\geq(i{-})$ by $i$ then applying antitonicity yields $bb=b$.
Since $\ident\leq{-}{-}\leq{-}i{-}=b$ and $b$ is clearly non-decreasing,
we conclude that $b$ is a closure operator on $L$.

For brevity we write ``$o_1\leq o_2$ left-multiplied [right-multiplied] by $o_3$'' as
``$o_3(o_1\leq o_2)$ $[(o_1\leq o_2)o_3]$'' and ``edge $i$ $\Longleftarrow$'' as ``$i:$''.
Edges $1$, $22$, $28$, $32$, $37$, $38$, $42$ hold by Proposition~$2.1$
in \cite{2025_ciraulo} and antitonicity.
Proposition~$2.1$ also implies:
$16:(i\leq ibi){-}{-}$, $29:(\ident\leq b){-}$, $30:{-}i[(\ident\leq b)i{-}{-}]$,
$44:(ibi\leq ib){-}$, $46:(i\leq ibi){-}$.
Note that $34:[{-}(22)]bi{-}{-}$ (numbers represent previously obtained edges).
The rest now follow sequentially:
$2:{-}(37)$, 
$3:(2)ib$,
$4:bi(2)$,
$5:(37){-}$,
$7:(6)ib$,
$8:{-}{-}i(4)$,
$9:{-}(34)$,
$10:bi(6)$,
$11:i(4)$,
$12:(6)ibi{-}{-}$,
$13:{-}(30)$,
$14:{-}{-}ibi(6)$,
$15:{-}(32)$,
