The external gravitational perturbation $K^+$ is a solution of the Laplace equation \eqref{ggg6}. It reads (c.f. Eqs. \eqref{oin6}, \eqref{oi4zs}),
\bal{2dc}
K^+&=&-\frac23\rho_0{\mathsf K}_0\q\sum_{{l}=0}^\infty\left[A_l\left(\frac{\xi}{\xi_1}\right)^{-l-1}+B_l\left(\frac{\xi}{\xi_1}\right)^l\right]P_{l}(\cos\theta)\;. 
\ea
Its radial derivative is
\bal{2dd}
K'^+&=&-\frac23\sigma_0{\mathsf K}_0\q\sum_{{l}=0}^\infty\left[-(l+1)\frac{A_l}{\xi_1}\left(\frac{\xi}{\xi_1}\right)^{-l-2}+l\frac{B_l}{\xi_1}\left(\frac{\xi}{\xi_1}\right)^{l-1}\right]P_{l}(\cos\theta)\;,
\ea
where the prime denotes a derivative with respect to the coordinate $\xi$, and the constant coefficients
\bal{2de}
A_l=k_l\;,\qquad\qquad B_l=\delta_{2l}\;.
\ea
Matching the internal and external solutions for the gravitational field perturbation and their radial derivatives at the spherical boundary $\xi=\xi_1$, gives two boundary conditions:
\bal{2df}
k_l+\delta_{2l}&=&-c_lj_l(\xi_1)\qquad\quad,\quad\qquad
(l+1)k_l-l\delta_{2l}=c_l\xi_1j'_l(\xi_1)\;,
\ea
which are valid up to the residual terms of the order of ${\cal O}({\mathsf q})$. 

Solution of this system of equations $\forall l\neq 2$ is $k_l={\cal O}({\mathsf q})$, $c_l={\cal O}({\mathsf q}^2)$ and
\bal{2dh}
k_2&=&\frac{2-\xi_1y(\xi_1)}{3+\xi_1 y(\xi_1)}\qquad\quad,\quad\qquad c_2=-\frac{5}{3j_2(\xi_1)+\xi_1j'_2(\xi_1)}\;,
\ea
where $y(\xi_1):=j'_2(\xi_1)/j_2(\xi_1)$. Substituting the boundary value of the radial coordinate $\xi_1=\pi$ and calculating the values of the Love number $k_2$ and the expansion coefficient $c_2$ yields
\bal{2dj}
k_2&=&-1+\frac{15}{\pi^2}\qquad\quad,\quad\qquad c_2=-5\;,
\ea
where we have dropped off the residual terms.
This yields the final solution for $K^-$, which is
\bal{dst7}
K^-&=&c_2 j_2(\xi)P_2(\cos\theta)\;.
\ea






