We confirm that the master equation \eqref{xc9a} was correctly derived using an alternative approach. The coordinate transformation \eqref{d1q23} along with its inverse, $x^i=y^i-X^i$, provide the transformation law for partial derivatives,
\bal{ki65}
\frac{\pd}{\pd x^i}&=&M_{ij}\frac{\pd}{\pd y^j}\qquad\longleftrightarrow \qquad\frac{\pd}{\pd y^i}=M^{-1}_{ij}\frac{\pd}{\pd x^j}\;.
\ea
This allows us to express the master equation \eqref{xc9a} as the Poisson equation for the undisturbed potential $U$ in the $y^i$ coordinates,
\bal{kn75}
\Delta_y\tilde{U}({\bm y})=-4\pi G\tilde\sigma({\bm y})\;,
\ea
where $\Delta_y:=\delta^{ij}\pd^2/\pd y^i\pd y^j$ is the Laplace operator in ${\bm y}=(y^i)$ coordinates, the density $\tilde\sigma({\bm y})=\sigma({\bm y}-{\bm X})$ and the potential $\tilde U({\bm y})=U({\bm y}-{\bm X})$ which are functions of the ${\bm y}$ coordinates and ${\bm X}={\bm X}({\bm y})$. On the other hand, Eq. \eqref{kn75} can be derived directly from the primary condition \eqref{c3}, which establishes a correspondence between the perturbed and unperturbed level surfaces. We can re-formulate Eq. \eqref{c3} in terms of the deformed coordinates ${\bm y}$,
\bal{cxv54}
V({\bm y})=U\left({\bm y}-{\bm X}\right)\;,
\ea
where ${\bm X}={\bm X}({\bm y})$ is a function of ${\bm y}$. By applying the Laplace operator $\Delta_y$ to both sides of this equations and noting that $U({\bm y}-{\bm X})=\tilde U({\bm y})$, we obtain,
\bal{mub32x}
\Delta_y\tilde{U}({\bm y})=\Delta_y V({\bm y})\;.
\ea
The right-hand side of this equation is computed using Eq. \eqref{bxg655}, which is form-invariant under the substitution of different names for independent variables, 
\bal{655eq}
\Delta_y V({\bm y})&=&-4\pi G\left[\sigma({\bm y})+\varrho({\bm y})\right]\;.
\ea
The right-hand side is transformed with the help of Eq. \eqref{ikr9}, which, after variable replacement, becomes
\bal{i9as}
\sigma({\bm y})+\varrho({\bm y})&=&\sigma({\bm y}-{\bm X})\;.
\ea
Substituting this equation to the right-hand side of Eq. \eqref{655eq} yields,
\bal{01eq}
\Delta_y V({\bm y})&=&-4\pi G\sigma({\bm y}-{\bm X})\;.
\ea
Recalling that $\sigma({\bm y}-{\bm X})=\tilde\sigma({\bm y})$, we can replace Eq. \eqref{01eq} in the right-hand side of Eq. \eqref{mub32x}, leading us to Eq. \eqref{kn75}. Q.E.D.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The integration of the Laplacian is done using the Gauss theorem. The other terms are integrated using the the continuity of the gravitational potential $U$ and its perturbation $K$, and the continuity of the radial derivative $U'$. Outside the body the perturbation $K$ obey the Laplace equation \eqref{jk6v7}. Therefore, integration of Eq. \eqref{fe87} over the infinitesimally small volume yields:
\bal{vcr5}
K'^+(a,\theta)&=&K'^-(a,\theta)-4\pi G\left\{K^-(a,\theta)\lim_{\ve\to 0}\int_a^{a+\ve}\frac{dr}{A}+\sum_{n=2}\frac{\left[K^-(a,\theta)\right]^l}{l!}\lim_{\ve\to 0}\int_a^{a+\ve}\frac{g_l}{A^l}dr\right\}\;.
\ea
Explicit calculation of all integrals in the right-hand side of Eq. \eqref{vcr5} seems analytically unfeasible due to the complicated nature of the coeffcients $g_l$ defined in Eq. \eqref{s10}. Hence, we restrict ourselves only with the calculation of the first few terms using Eqs. \eqref{s13}--\eqref{s14}. We have:
\bal{mmm1}
\int_a^{a+\ve}\frac{dr}{A}&=&\frac{\sigma(a)}{U'(a)}\;,\\
\lim_{\ve\to 0}\int_a^{a+\ve}\frac{g_2}{A^2}dr&=&\frac{1}{U'^2(a)}\left[\sigma'(a)+\frac{2\sigma(a)}{a}+\frac{2\pi G\sigma^2(a)}{U'(a)}\right]\;,\\
\lim_{\ve\to 0}\int_a^{a+\ve}\frac{g_3}{A^3}dr&=&\frac{1}{U'^3(a)}\left[\sigma''(a) +\frac{6\sigma'(a)}{a}+\frac{6\sigma(a)}{a^2}+\frac{12\pi G\sigma(a)\sigma'(a)}{U'(a)}+\frac{20\pi G\sigma^2(a)}{aU'(a)}+\frac{16\pi^2G^2\sigma^3(a)}{U'^2(a)}\right]\;,
\ea
and so on.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection{Boundary Conditions for the Height Function}

The boundary conditions \eqref{m5ez} and \eqref{bhyh} are used to derive the boundary conditions imposed on the height function $X(\x)$ at the boundary surface $r=a$. Inside the body, the gravitational perturbation $K^-(\x)$ is given by equation \eqref{bv4rd6}. The condition \eqref{m5ez} of the continuity of the gravitational field perturbation, when substituted into Eq. \eqref{bv4rd6}, immediately provides an equation to determine the height function on the boundary of the body:
\bal{bv12}
K^+(a,\theta)&=&\sum_{n=1}^\infty\left(-\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\right)^nU(a)\;,
\ea
where ${\bm X}=X{\bm n}$, $X=X(a,\theta)$ and $U(a)$ is the unperturbed gravitational potential \eqref{omd6bb} on the surface of the reference configuration $r=a$.

The second boundary condition is derived using Eq. \eqref{xc7}, which can be also represented as follows: 
\bal{xc7aa}
\left(1+\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\right)\pd_i K^-({\bm x})&=&M^{-1}_{ij}({\bm X})\pd_j U({\bm x})-\left(1+\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\right)\pd_i U({\bm x})\;.
\ea
We multiply both sides of \eqref{xc7aa} by the unit vector $n^i$ and take into account that it commutes in the radial gauge with the shift operator, i.e., $n^i\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}=\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X} n^i$. This gives,
\bal{gh76v}
\left(1+\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\right)K'^-({\bm x})&=&n^iM^{-1}_{ij}({\bm X})\pd_j U({\bm x})-\left(1+\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\right)U'({\bm x})\;,
\ea
where the prime denotes a partial derivative with respect to the radial coordinate.
Here, the first term on the right-hand side of \eqref{xc7aa} is calculated using \eqref{ek8}:
\bal{pop865}
n^iM^{-1}_{ij}({\bm X})\pd_j U({\bm x})&=&\frac{U'}{1+X'}\;.
\ea
Thus, Eq. \eqref{gh76v} becomes
\bal{xc7bb}
\left(1+\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\right)K'^-&=&\frac{U'}{1+X'}-\left(1+\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\right)U'\;.
\ea
The last term in this equation is:
\bal{po4567}
\left(1+\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\right)U'&=&\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{X^n}{n!}\pd^n_r\left[-\frac{GM(r)}{r^2}\right]=-G\left(\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{X^n}{n!}\pd_r^nr^{-2}\right)\left(\sum_{p=0}^\infty\frac{X^p}{p!}\pd_r^p M(r)\right)\\\nonumber
&=& \frac{r^2}{(r + X)^2}U'(r)-\frac{G}{(r+X)^2}\sum_{p=1}^\infty\frac{X^p}{p!}\pd_r^p M(r)\;,
\ea
where the terms with the derivatives from the mass $M(r)$ inside the sphere of radius $r$ depend on the density and its radial derivatives which are different from zero in the most general case.
Substituting this result back into Eq. \eqref{xc7bb} and reducing similar terms, we get:
\bal{xc7cc}
K'^-&=&\sum_{n=0}^\infty\left(-\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\right)^n\left\{\left[\frac{1}{1+X'}-\frac{r^2}{(r + X)^2}\right]U'(r)+\frac{G}{(r+X)^2}\sum_{p=1}^\infty\frac{X^p}{p!}\pd_r^p M(r)\right\}\;.
\ea
After expanding the fractions in the square brackets into a Taylor series, we obtain:
\bal{xc7qq}
K'^-&=&\sum_{n=0}^\infty\sum_{k=0}^\infty(-1)^{k}\left(-\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\right)^n\left\{\left[X'^k- (k+1)\frac{X^k}{r^k}\right]U'(r)+(k+1)\frac{GX^k}{r^{k+2}}\sum_{p=1}^\infty\frac{X^p}{p!}\pd_r^p M(r)\right\}\;.
\ea
Applying the boundary condition \eqref{bhyh} for the radial derivative of the gravitational field perturbation to Eq. \eqref{xc7qq}, we derive the second boundary condition imposed on the height function $X(\x)=X(r,\theta)$:
\bal{h5c2l}\hspace{-0.5cm}
K'^+(a,\theta)&=&\sum_{n=0}^\infty\sum_{k=0}^\infty(-1)^{k}\left(-\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\right)^n\left[X'^k- (k+1)\frac{X^k}{a^k}\right]U'(a)\\\nonumber
&+&\sum_{n=0}^\infty\sum_{k=0}^\infty(-1)^k(k+1)\left(-\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\right)^n\frac{GX^k}{a^{k+2}}\sum_{p=1}^\infty\frac{X^p}{p!}\frac{\pd^pM(a)}{\pd a^p} 
- \sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{4\pi G X^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\frac{\pd^n}{\pd a^n}\sum_{p=0}^\infty\left(-\hat{\mathsf T}_{{\bm X}}\right)^p\sigma(a)\;,
\ea
where ${\bm X}=X{\bm n}$, $X=X(a,\theta)$, and all radial derivatives $\pd_r=\pd_a$ are taken on the surface of the body $r=a$. The terms with the derivatives from $M(a)$ are proportional to the surface density of the body and its radial derivatives which do not vanish on the body's surface in the most general case and must be taken into account. 

The boundary conditions \eqref{bv12} and \eqref{h5c2l} imposed on the height function $X(\x)$ are given in the form of infinite power series,  making their exact solution unfeasible. Nonetheless, an approximate solution can be obtained straightforwardly by iterations. In this paper, we limit ourselves to the quadratic approximation which is developed below for the radial spectral harmonics of the height and shape functions. 

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
Eq. \eqref{mom2}  can be re-written in the following form:
\bal{mom4}
\mu(\x)&=&\rho(r)-\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}\mu(\x)\;,
\ea
which can be further developed by using definition \eqref{asrx5} of the shift operator $\hat{\mathsf T}_{\bm X}$:
\bal{mom5}
\mu(\x)&=&\rho(r)-\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{X^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\frac{\pd^{n+1}\mu(\x)}{\pd r^{n+1}}\;.
\ea
Substituting thsi expression into the first term in the right-hand side of Eq. \eqref{mom3} and integrating by parts, we find out that the integrals entering Eq. \eqref{mom3} cancel out each other, and the multipole moment $\M_l$ is given by:
\bal{mom6}
\M_l&=&\int_\V\rho(r)r^{l+2}P_l(\cos\theta)drd^2\Omega({\bm n})+\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{1}{(n+1)!}\int_\V\frac{\pd^n\mu(\x)}{\pd r^n}P_l(\cos\theta)d\biggl[r^{l+2}X^{n+1}(\x)\biggr]d^2\Omega({\bm n})\\
\nonumber&+&\oint_{{\mathbb S}^2}d^2\Omega({\bm n}_a)P_l\left(\cos\theta_a\right)\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{X^{n+1}({\bm a})}{(n+1)!}\left\{\frac{\pd^n}{\pd a^n} \biggl[\mu({\bm a})a^{l+2}\biggr]-a^{l+2}\frac{\pd^n\mu({\bm a})}{\pd a^n}\right\}\;.
  \ea

