Figure \ref{fig:fig2} presents the plan-view SEM and TEM images of EuBCO films with 0 and 2.8 vol\% BHO, highlighting their grain structures. The grain boundaries of PLD-grown REBCO films contain high densities of dislocations \cite{Miura2006}. Arrows in the SEM images in Figs. \ref{fig:fig2}(a, d) highlight example grain widths, exemplifying how the average grain size in the film containing BHO is about twice that of the films without BHO.  Consequently, given the smaller grain sizes, the films without BHO contain a higher density of dislocations than those with BHO. The large ($\sim$1000 nm) grains appearing in the SEM images likely correspond to misoriented grains. Similarly sized misoriented grains were identified in a TEM study of 3300 nm thick EuBCO films \cite{Zhao2025}. The corresponding TEM images, Figs. \ref{fig:fig2}(b, e), reveal twin boundaries that appear as striation patterns within individual grains. To highlight the main differences in the defect landscape created by incorporating BHO inclusions, Figs. \ref{fig:fig2}(c, f) illustrate both microstructures from three different perspectives: 3D, $ab$-plane slices, and $ca$-plane slices.  


